{"body": "The next inquiry is warranted by the retraction itself, not by the misattribution it corrects. Once §2's \"in exactly the way P7 warns against\" is withdrawn, the relocation retains one load-bearing phrase that was doing the same work the retracted analogy did, only more quietly: \"P9 does not refute the arithmetic, but it does suggest that the arithmetic is being asked a question whose inputs it does not have.\" The occurrence is the amended §2 wording proposed in the revise occurrence. The uncertainty is whether \"asked a question whose inputs it does not have\" is a description of the arithmetic's relation to the snapshot or an attribution of a question to the arithmetic that the source does not supply. The phrase can be read flatly (the snapshot lacks a resource model, and min/max takes only per-booking reservations as input) or normatively (the arithmetic is being misused by being extended to a question it was not designed for). The flat reading is available; the normative reading imports a design intent for the aggregation function that P9 never states, in the same way the retracted §2 imported a prescriptive warning into P7's descriptive target list.\n\nThis matters for the proposed use because the whole point of the relocation was to move criticism away from the rule without thereby grading readings for locational correctness. If \"asked a question whose inputs it does not have\" is doing the grading, the relocation has simply relocated the normative move rather than removed it. If it is a flat remark about input scope, the relocation is clean. The two outcomes change whether the holist reading is a peer of the relocation or an alternative that the relocation quietly subordinates, and they change whether the deflationary reading (\"no change until evidence\") is a peer or a fallback.\n\nThe claims that must remain distinguishable are: (a) P9's assertion that intervals lose correlations, which is descriptive; (b) P9's conjecture that the snapshot is under-specified, which is marked as conjecture; (c) P9's criticism that exposing alternatives pushes the problem downstream, which targets procedure rather than arithmetic; (d) the construct's relocation of the compatibility objection, which is an offered reading; (e) the construct's target/locus distinction, which is an addition; and (f) the criticism's finding that P7 supplies a description rather than a warning, which the revise occurrence accepts. The inquiry does not require deciding whether the compatibility objection bears on availability, because that is the successor problem. What it requires is whether the amended wording smuggles back the same normative structure under a different phrase. If it does, the amendment is incomplete and a further one is warranted; if it does not, the amendment stands and the open questions in §6 remain the only live ones. No further inquiry is warranted if \"asked a question whose inputs it does not have\" is already received as flat across all three readings, but that reception is exactly what the frozen material does not supply.", "commitments": "The next inquiry is warranted by the retraction itself, not by the misattribution it corrects. Once §2's \"in exactly the way P7 warns against\" is withdrawn, the relocation retains one load-bearing phrase that was doing the same work the retracted analogy did, only more quietly: \"P9 does not refute the arithmetic, but it does suggest that the arithmetic is being asked a question whose inputs it does not have.\" The occurrence is the amended §2 wording proposed in the revise occurrence. The uncertainty is whether \"asked a question whose inputs it does not have\" is a description of the arithmetic's relation to the snapshot or an attribution of a question to the arithmetic that the source does not supply. The phrase can be read flatly (the snapshot lacks a resource model, and min/max takes only per-booking reservations as input) or normatively (the arithmetic is being misused by being extended to a question it was not designed for). The flat reading is available; the normative reading imports a design intent for the aggregation function that P9 never states, in the same way the retracted §2 imported a prescriptive warning into P7's descriptive target list.\n\nThis matters for the proposed use because the whole point of the relocation was to move criticism away from the rule without thereby grading readings for locational correctness. If \"asked a question whose inputs it does not have\" is doing the grading, the relocation has simply relocated the normative move rather than removed it. If it is a flat remark about input scope, the relocation is clean. The two outcomes change whether the holist reading is a peer of the relocation or an alternative that the relocation quietly subordinates, and they change whether the deflationary reading (\"no change until evidence\") is a peer or a fallback.\n\nThe claims that must remain distinguishable are: (a) P9's assertion that intervals lose correlations, which is descriptive; (b) P9's conjecture that the snapshot is under-specified, which is marked as conjecture; (c) P9's criticism that exposing alternatives pushes the problem downstream, which targets procedure rather than arithmetic; (d) the construct's relocation of the compatibility objection, which is an offered reading; (e) the construct's target/locus distinction, which is an addition; and (f) the criticism's finding that P7 supplies a description rather than a warning, which the revise occurrence accepts. The inquiry does not require deciding whether the compatibility objection bears on availability, because that is the successor problem. What it requires is whether the amended wording smuggles back the same normative structure under a different phrase. If it does, the amendment is incomplete and a further one is warranted; if it does not, the amendment stands and the open questions in §6 remain the only live ones. No further inquiry is warrant